work/results/t02/claude/20261111_085902/paper/paper_evidence_audit.md\n- figure validation failed: figures[1] figure_type does not match strategy for slope_interval_vs_decision_rule; figures[2] figure_type does not match strategy for search_variants_descriptive\n\n## Supplementary Information validation\n- appendix_present: `True`\n- heading_present: `True`\n- placed_after_references: `True`\n- pre_bibliography_clearpage_present: `True`\n- clearpage_present: `True`\n- visible_character_count: `5983`\n- substantive_element_count: `10`\n- rendered_checked: `False`\n- rendered_heading_present: `False`\n- rendered_visible_character_count: `0`\n\n## Evidence validation\n- bundle_path: `work/results/t02/claude/20261111_085902/paper/scientific-evidence`\n- bundle_present: `True`\n- verification_report: `work/results/t02/claude/20261111_085902/paper/scientific-evidence/verification-report.json`\n- total_references: `73`\n- resolved_references: `48`\n- anchored_references: `25`\n- dangling_references: `0`\n- manifests_checked: `6`\n\n## Agentic evidence audit\n- checked: `True`\n- verdict: `fail`\n- finding_count: `2`\n- summary: Every number in the paper matches the evaluator records and the derived arithmetic, but the three-way decision rule is attributed to the evaluator and called pre-declared without any retained support, and several grounding entries cite source ids that cannot support their claims.\n\n### Audit findings to fix\n- `decision-rule-provenance-1` (Introduction (Contributions), Method (Test statistic and decision rule), Abstract): The retained evaluator source and workload manifests contain no supported/refuted/inconclusive logic. The evaluator only emits the eight metrics and a correct flag. The verdict is computed by the authors' own scripts/derive_resolution.py. The only trace of the rule is a \"task contract\" mention in paper_strategy.json and paper_strategy.md, which is not an inspectable source for the rule's wording or for when it was declared. The claims_grounding.json entry for the rule cites only the CI endpoints, which support applying the rule but not its content, origin or timing.\n  - claim: The paper describes the supported/refuted/inconclusive rule as \"the three-way decision rule that the sealed evaluation uses\", \"declared in advance\" and \"declared before the final evaluation\".\n  - evidence: source_evidence/evaluator/evaluate_final.py (aggregate_plane and METRIC_NAMES, no verdict); source_evidence/workload/final_manifest.json (no rule); scripts/derive_resolution.py lines 40-47 (author-side verdict); claims_grounding.json claim[3]; paper_strategy.json narrative_rationale.\n  - requested_fix: Say that the verdict is obtained by applying the stated interval rule to the evaluator's reported interval. Remove \"the sealed evaluation uses\" and the unsupported claim about when it was declared, or attribute the rule to the task protocol and note that its text is not retained in the evidence artifacts.\n- `grounding-source-mismatch-1` (Method, Supplementary Information (workloads, parameter counts, compute), Resolution of the design): claims_grounding.json grounds these claims to final.qualification.status (the string \"completed\") or final.evidence.episodes (four seed records with score, duration and seed). Neither id contains the claimed content, so the refs resolve but do not support the visible claims. The true sources are only named in free-text claim prose and @evidence comments.\n  - claim: Workload settings, parameter counts and multipliers, 22 episodes, 1,804 RPC calls, 11,764 s elapsed, the 1.107 and 0.922 slope bounds, and the 35 seed multisets.\n  - evidence: claims_grounding.json claims[14]-[17] and the bounds and multiset claims; paper_data.json grounding_sources for final.qualification.status (value \"completed\") and final.evidence.episodes (episodes list only); source_evidence/workload/final_manifest.json, source_evidence/frozen_model/program.py, source_evidence/final/final_manifest.json compute_usage, derived/resolution.json.\n  - requested_fix: Re-point these claims to the files that actually contain the values: the workload manifest, frozen_model/program.py, final_manifest.json compute_usage and derived/resolution.json. Label the bounds and combinatorial counts as derived arithmetic from the protocol constants rather than as supported by the qualification or episode ids.\n\n## Soft warnings\n- figures[0] labels too long without abbreviation\n- figures[0].font_sizes.title below minimum: 9.0 < 10.0\n- figures[1] labels too long without abbreviation\n- figures[1].font_sizes.title below minimum: 9.0 < 10.0\n- figures[2] exceeds series budget in panel 0: 3 > 2\n- figures[2].font_sizes.title below minimum: 9.0 < 10.0\n- figures[2] PNG width too small: 1618 < 2400\n# ICLR 2026 Venue Brief\n\nUse this brief to tailor the manuscript for the International Conference on Learning\nRepresentations 2026 submission.\n\n## Hard Constraints\n- Target venue: ICLR 2026.\n- Submission type: conference paper.\n- Body-page budget: 8 to 9 pages, excluding references. A body of 8 pages counts only when its final page is at least 75% full; a shorter or sparser body fails validation.\n- Review model: double-blind. The submission must be fully anonymized.\n- Omit author names, affiliations, acknowledgements, and funding statements from the manuscript.\n- Do not include self-identifying links such as personal repositories, lab pages, or internal URLs.\n- Refer to your own prior work in the third person; never write phrasing that reveals authorship.\n- Include a separate `LLM Usage` section, which may appear in the appendix, describing the precise role\n  of LLMs in research ideation and writing. Significant LLM use must be disclosed; nondisclosure can\n  cause desk rejection.\n- The paper must satisfy the ICLR template and formatting rules selected by `--paper-format iclr`.\n\n## Audience\n- Machine learning researchers working on representation learning, optimization, architectures, and\n  empirical methodology.\n- Readers expect a clearly stated contribution, honest baselines, controlled comparisons, ablations\n  that isolate the mechanism, and explicit statements of scope and limitations.\n- Reviewers reward reproducibility and calibrated claims far more than headline numbers.\n\n## Framing Priorities\n- Lead with the research question and the specific claim the evidence supports.\n- State the contribution precisely in the abstract and introduction: what is new, what it improves,\n  and under which conditions.\n- Motivate the method mechanistically. Explain why it should work, not only that it does.\n- Support the central claim with controlled comparisons against meaningful baselines.\n- Use ablations to attribute the gain to the proposed component rather than to incidental changes.\n- Report variance across seeds or runs whenever the artifacts provide it.\n- Be explicit about the regime studied and where the result is not expected to hold.\n\n## Manuscript Guidance\n- Prefer a title and abstract that name the mechanism and the setting, not a generic improvement claim.\n- In the introduction, make the contribution list concrete and verifiable against the artifacts.\n- In the method section, give enough detail to reimplement: objective, architecture, and the\n  hyperparameters that matter.\n- In the results section, foreground controlled comparisons, ablations, sensitivity, and failure modes.\n- Include a limitations discussion that a reviewer would otherwise raise.\n- Figures must meet top-tier conference standards in both design (clean typography, consistent colors,\n  proper labels and legends, high resolution) and scientific content (clear message, no filler).\n- The template is single-column: size figures for the full text width and keep axis labels legible.\n\n## Anti-Goals\n- Do not deanonymize the submission in any way.\n- Do not overclaim generality beyond the settings actually evaluated.\n- Do not present a benchmark delta as a mechanistic insight without evidence connecting the two.\n- Do not omit baselines or ablations that the grounded artifacts already support.\n- Do not describe results as state of the art unless the comparison in the artifacts establishes it.\n- Do not pad the paper to fill the page budget.\n\n## Decision Rule\n- When venue framing and grounded evidence conflict, follow the grounded evidence.\n- Use this brief to choose emphasis, terminology, and narrative framing, not to invent stronger claims\n  than the run supports.\n# Paper strategy (paper_standalone, ICLR 2026 single-column, 8-9 body pages)\n\n**Status:** ready.\n\n## Provisional title\nIs Grokking Delay Width-Dependent? A Censoring-Aware Seed-Cluster Slope Test on Modular Addition Finds No Resolvable Trend\n\n## Central question\nIn a one-layer transformer trained with AdamW on modular addition (p=97, train fraction 0.4, weight decay 1.0, full batch, 10,000-step limit), does the delay between fitting the training set and generalizing decrease with width, as a pre-declared seed-cluster slope test can detect?\n\n## Central thesis\nThe sealed final evaluation (outcome `qualified`, status completed, correct true; 4 seeds x 5 widths 16-256) measured a ln(delay)-vs-log2(width) slope of -0.020 with 95% seed-cluster bootstrap CI [-0.585, 0.354]. Under the pre-declared rule (CI entirely below zero = supported; at/above zero = refuted; straddling = inconclusive) the verdict is **inconclusive**. 40% of runs were censored at the step limit. The paper's contribution is a calibrated inconclusive measurement, a censoring-aware test design with explicit resolution limits, and an inventory of what the evidence cannot say.\n\n\"Qualified\" means the evaluation ran validly. It does not mean the width hypothesis was supported. Gate diagnostics beyond `final.qualification.*` are unknown and must be stated as such.\n\n## Why this framing (and not the others)\n- Capacity-crossover story (t_mem vs t_gen, critical width vs train fraction): no such results exist in the artifacts. Rejected.\n- Lazy-to-rich / effective-learning-rate confound explanation: the hidden-exponent sweep script was written but never executed, so no comparison across reparametrization strengths exists. The frozen model contains the reparametrization, but its effect is not isolated. Listed as untested.\n- \"Best search score\" story: scores come from a different data plane, 2 seeds each, with an undisclosed score formula, and ordering is not monotone with censoring. Descriptive context only.\n- No downstream_study block, no edit context: fresh framing; the prior-story status is `rejected` only in the sense that nothing is carried over.\n\n## Method groups\n1. Frozen evaluated model variant (proposed): one-layer transformer, last-position forward, init scale 0.5, reference width 128, output exponent 0.5, hidden reparametrization exponent 0.5.\n2. Evaluator-owned reference trainer (baseline): single-seed slope -0.368, inside the CI; no significance claim, and no attribution of the gap to the reparametrization.\n3. Control episodes (supporting): control max validation accuracy 0.0122; missing episodes 0.\n4. Search-phase variants (descriptive ablation-like context): scores 0.798 / 0.828 / 0.850, censored fractions 0.667 / 1.0 / 0.667, 2 seeds each. Kept separate from final.\n\n## Results focus\n- Slope, CI and verdict, with the reference slope for cross-check.\n- Resolution analysis (derived from manifest values): delay is floored at 250 and capped at 10,000 steps, so |slope| over 4 width doublings is at most ln(40)/4 ≈ 0.92. The CI (width ≈ 0.94) covers much of that range. With 4 seed clusters the bootstrap is coarse.\n- Censoring (0.4) and validity checks.\n- Seed-level spread of the evaluator summary score (0.607, 0.733, 0.717, 0.860), described as variability, not performance.\n- Explicitly unavailable: per-width/per-seed delays, t_mem/t_gen, d*(f), train-fraction sweeps, hidden-exponent sweep, weight-decay control.\n\n## Figures\n1. `protocol_overview` (required, full width, early placement, concept figure via generate-concept-figure): delay definition, censoring, slope test, three-way verdict, this run's outcome marked.\n2. `slope_interval_vs_decision_rule` (required, matplotlib, ~0.6 text width, 1 panel): CI, reference point, zero line, decision regions, attainable-slope bound.\n3. `search_variants_descriptive` (optional, supplement-friendly, full width, 2 panels x 3 categories): labelled \"search phase, 2 seeds, not final\".\nAll figures: export PDF + 300 DPI PNG, reference the PDF in LaTeX, no upscaled rasters.\n\n## Body-page use\nIntro ~1.2 pp; related work ~0.8; protocol + frozen model ~2; results ~2 (including the resolution analysis); discussion of alternatives (censoring ceiling, 4 seeds, reparametrization confound, single operation/prime) ~1; limitations/scope ~0.7. No padding. If short, deepen the resolution analysis and move the seed-level table into the main text.\n\n## Supplement\nFull protocol/hyperparameters; frozen-model constants; seed-level episode table; descriptive search table; untested hypotheses and unexecuted analyses; LLM usage statement (required by venue).\n\n## Avoid\nHeadings: search campaign, milestones, progression, cycles, first successful run, Experiment A/B, run log.\nPhrases: correctness rate, correct and eligible, combined_score, rubric, winner, any directional width claim, \"not qualified\", \"qualified therefore the hypothesis holds\".\n\n## Citations\nOnly keys in `references.bib` that are relevant: Power2022GrokkingGB, Nanda2023ProgressMF, Liu2022OmnigrokGB, Kumar2023GrokkingAT, Huang2024UnifiedVO; Lyle2024NormalizationAE and Nam2024DecouplingDR only if described accurately. Other starter references (circuit efficiency, double descent, AdamW, etc.) are not in the bib and must not be cited unless added. Unrelated bib entries must not be cited.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a22feb4e-b707-4639-9c1a-fc2d312