{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HtQQzQtB8FHw9VL6qmaBSW","type":"tool_result","content":"# Draft reference gap memo\n\n**Status:** ready. A targeted post-draft retrieval pass is justified for three paragraphs. The rest of the draft is either well cited or grounded in `paper_data.json`.\n\n## What is already adequate\n- The grokking explanations in the introduction and the first Related Work paragraph (Power, Nanda, Liu, Varma, Kumar, Huang, Davies) are cited directly.\n- All quantitative results (slope, interval, censored fraction, resolution limits) are grounded in the evaluator artifacts and need no citations.\n\n## Targets\n\n### 1. Related work, \"Parametrization and optimizer scale\" (high, background)\nThe evaluated model's key design element is a width-dependent multiplier meant to equalize the effective step size under Adam. The Discussion argues this could mask or create a width slope. The paragraph cites only an RL normalization paper and a dynamical-richness measurement paper. The standard width-aware parametrization literature is missing.\n- Search topics: muP / Tensor Programs; hyperparameter transfer across width; Adam parametrization and effective step size.\n\n### 2. Related work and introduction, prior work on width dependence (high, positioning)\nThe paper asks how grokking delay depends on model width. No cited work is positioned as having examined delay against width, size, weight decay, or initialization scale. The \"Relation to this work\" paragraph and the capacity-based reading both need this positioning. The memorization and double-descent citations are only indirect support.\n- Search topics: grokking on modular arithmetic versus width and weight decay; grokking time versus model or data size; initialization scale and effective learning rate in grokking.\n\n### 3. Method, test statistic (medium, background)\nThe statistic is a seed-cluster percentile bootstrap on delays right-censored at the cap, entered at the cap value. The Discussion recommends censored-data analysis. No methodological reference supports either choice or the remedy.\n- Search topics: cluster bootstrap with few clusters; Tobit / accelerated-failure-time regression; bias from imputing censored values.\n\n## Not requested\nStronger citations for memorization, double descent, or the individual grokking explanations. These are adequately covered and would only enlarge the bibliography.\npostdraft_citation_weave_agent_claude_20261111_140816.log\npostdraft_citation_weave_prompt.md\npostdraft_reference_query_plan.json\npostdraft_reference_query_plan.md\npostdraft_reference_query_plan_agent_claude_20261111_140816.log\npostdraft_reference_query_plan_prompt.md\npostdraft_reference_query_plan_validation_feedback.json\npostdraft_reference_query_plan_validation_feedback.md\npostdraft_reference_retrieval.log\npostdraft_references.bib\npostdraft_references_provenance.json\n# Post-draft reference query plan\n\n**Status:** ready. The gap memo justifies a targeted pass on three paragraphs, giving six queries. Nothing else in the draft needs new citations.\n\n**Central topic:** how grokking delay on modular addition depends on transformer width. The gaps are the parametrization literature, prior empirical grokking work, and statistical methodology.\n\n## Already covered (not searched)\nPower, Nanda, Liu (Omnigrok), Varma, Kumar, Huang and Davies on grokking explanations. Memorization and double-descent citations (Zhang, Nakkiran, Tirumala, Morris). AdamW (Loshchilov). Lyle (RL effective learning rates) and Nam (dynamical richness) are cited but do not cover the width parametrization literature. Quantitative results are grounded in the evaluator artifacts and need no citations.\n\n## Queries\n\n### Gap 1: Related work, parametrization and optimizer scale (high)\n1. `maximal update parametrization feature learning infinite-width neural networks Tensor Programs` (architectural_shift). This is the muP construction behind the width-dependent multipliers.\n2. `hyperparameter transfer across width zero-shot tuning Adam learning rate scaling parametrization` (architectural_shift). It supports the effective-step-size argument under Adam used in the Discussion.\n\n### Gap 2: Prior work on width dependence (high)\n3. `grokking weight decay training data fraction time to generalization modular arithmetic` (baseline_comparator). It positions the paper against earlier empirical grokking studies.\n4. `grokking initialization scale effective learning rate weight norm delayed generalization` (baseline_comparator). It covers the initialization-scale and effective-step-size confound.\n\nQueries 3 and 4 avoid \"model size/width and grokking delay\" phrasing. The first-stage plan flagged that phrasing as risking the excluded prior paper. Direct width-dependence work will only appear if it surfaces through these weight-decay, data-fraction and initialization queries.\n\n### Gap 3: Method, test statistic (medium)\n5. `cluster bootstrap inference with few clusters percentile confidence intervals` (core_method). It supports the seed-cluster percentile bootstrap.\n6. `regression with right-censored outcomes Tobit accelerated failure time bias of imputing censored values` (core_method). It supports the criticism of imputing the cap and the censored-analysis recommendation.\n\n## Avoid\nRe-searching the cited grokking explanations, memorization or double descent. Also avoid chronology or harness terms, LLM scaling-law queries, and explicit \"effect of model size on grokking delay\" phrasing.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a22feb4e-b707-4639-9c1a-fc2d3121b3c0","uuid":"d7211e07-843a-409d-803b-61c05325c98f","timestamp":"2026-11-11T14:52:18.066Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"# Draft reference gap memo\n\n**Status:** ready. A targeted post-draft retrieval pass is justified for three paragraphs. The rest of the draft is either well cited or grounded in `paper_data.json`.\n\n