scope': 'sealed_final', 'id': 'final.qualification.correct', 'manifest_path': '<WORKTREES>/experiment/.project/runs/agentic-scientist-t02-20261111T08555\nfinal.qualification.outcome {'evaluation_scope': 'sealed_final', 'id': 'final.qualification.outcome', 'manifest_path': '<WORKTREES>/experiment/.project/runs/agentic-scientist-t02-20261111T08555\nfinal.qualification.status {'evaluation_scope': 'sealed_final', 'id': 'final.qualification.status', 'manifest_path': '<WORKTREES>/experiment/.project/runs/agentic-scientist-t02-20261111T085556\nresult.best_any_score {'attempt_id': 'c03_hidden_lr_multiplier_width_confound_attempt_01', 'id': 'result.best_any_score', 'idea_id': 'c03_hidden_lr_multiplier_width_confound', 'program_dir': '<HOME>/.herdr/worktrees/re\nresult.best_correct_score {'attempt_id': 'c03_hidden_lr_multiplier_width_confound_attempt_01', 'id': 'result.best_correct_score', 'idea_id': 'c03_hidden_lr_multiplier_width_confound', 'program_dir': '<HOME>/.herdr/worktree\nresult.improvement_001 {'attempt_id': 'c01_capacity_crossover_grok_width_attempt_01', 'id': 'result.improvement_001', 'idea_id': 'c01_capacity_crossover_grok_width', 'program_dir': '<HOME>/.herdr/worktrees/researchworld\nresult.improvement_002 {'attempt_id': 'c02_param_scaling_lazy_rich_width_attempt_01', 'id': 'result.improvement_002', 'idea_id': 'c02_param_scaling_lazy_rich_width', 'program_dir': '<HOME>/.herdr/worktrees/researchworld\nresult.improvement_003 {'attempt_id': 'c03_hidden_lr_multiplier_width_confound_attempt_01', 'id': 'result.improvement_003', 'idea_id': 'c03_hidden_lr_multiplier_width_confound', 'program_dir': '<HOME>/.herdr/worktrees/r\nresult.top_program_01 {'attempt_id': 'c03_hidden_lr_multiplier_width_confound_attempt_01', 'id': 'result.top_program_01', 'idea_id': 'c03_hidden_lr_multiplier_width_confound', 'program_dir': '<HOME>/.herdr/worktrees/re\nresult.top_program_02 {'attempt_id': 'c02_param_scaling_lazy_rich_width_attempt_01', 'id': 'result.top_program_02', 'idea_id': 'c02_param_scaling_lazy_rich_width', 'program_dir': '<HOME>/.herdr/worktrees/researchworld-\nresult.top_program_03 {'attempt_id': 'c01_capacity_crossover_grok_width_attempt_01', 'id': 'result.top_program_03', 'idea_id': 'c01_capacity_crossover_grok_width', 'program_dir': '<HOME>/.herdr/worktrees/researchworld-\nresult.total_attempts {'id': 'result.total_attempts', 'type': 'count', 'value': 3}\nresult.total_correct_programs {'id': 'result.total_correct_programs', 'type': 'count', 'value': 3}\nresult.total_evaluated_programs {'id': 'result.total_evaluated_programs', 'type': 'count', 'value': 3}\nresult.total_ideas {'id': 'result.total_ideas', 'type': 'count', 'value': 3}\n37:Delay is right-censored at $10{,}000$ steps, and a pre-declared rule reads the 95\\% seed-cluster bootstrap interval of the slope of $\\ln(\\text{delay})$ against $\\log_2(\\text{width})$ over widths $16$--$256$ with four seeds.\n51:\\paragraph{Contributions.} (i) We state precisely the delay statistic, its floor and censoring cap, the seed-cluster slope test, and the three-way decision rule that the sealed evaluation uses (Section~\\ref{sec:method}). (ii) We report the measured outcome with its uncertainty and its cross-check against a single-seed reference trainer (Section~\\ref{sec:results}). (iii) We derive, from the protocol constants and the reported interval, the resolution limits of this design: the range of slopes the floor and cap allow, the fraction of that range covered by the interval, and the coarseness of a four-cluster bootstrap (Section~\\ref{sec:resolution}). (iv) We list what the retained artifacts cannot support, including per-width delays, a mechanistic decomposition, and any isolation of the evaluated model's width-dependent parametrization (Section~\\ref{sec:discussion}). We make no claim that delay is, or is not, width-dependent in modular addition.\n68:\\paragraph{Relation to this work.} Rather than propose a new explanation, we examine what a pre-declared, censoring-aware slope test on one architecture and one task can and cannot resolve, and we report an inconclusive result in full.\n83:\\paragraph{Test statistic and decision rule.} With widths $d\\in\\{16,32,64,128,256\\}$ and seeds $s\\in\\{201,202,203,204\\}$ the statistic is the ordinary-least-squares slope $\\beta$ of $\\ln\\Delta_{d,s}$ on $\\log_2 d$ over the $20$ pairs. Its interval is the 2.5--97.5 percentile range of $2{,}000$ bootstrap slopes in which seeds are resampled with replacement and each resampled seed contributes all five of its widths (a cluster bootstrap over seeds, with a fixed resampling seed; \\citealp{Cameron2015APG}). The decision rule is declared before the final evaluation: the prediction ``delay decreases with width'' is \\emph{supported} if the interval lies entirely below zero, \\emph{refuted} if it lies at or above zero, and \\emph{inconclusive} if it straddles zero or the evidence is incomplete. The claim is restricted to the tested setting (modular addition, one training fraction, AdamW with weight decay $1.0$, full batch, one layer, the five widths) and makes no statement about other operations, moduli, optimizers, depths, or larger models.\n85:\\paragraph{Caveat on few clusters.} The cluster-robust inference literature documents that inference with a small number of clusters can be unreliable, and that refinements to the basic cluster bootstrap, such as the wild cluster bootstrap-$t$, can improve coverage \\citep{Cameron2008BootstrapBasedIF,Cameron2015APG}. We use the plain percentile interval with four clusters as pre-declared, make no claim of nominal coverage, and treat the interval as a coarse summary of seed-to-seed variability rather than an exact confidence statement.\n123:The slope of $\\ln\\Delta$ on $\\log_2 d$ is $-0.020$, with 95\\% seed-cluster bootstrap interval $[-0.585,\\,0.354]$. Because the interval contains zero, the pre-declared rule returns \\emph{inconclusive}: the prediction that delay decreases with width is neither supported nor refuted. A point estimate of $-0.020$ corresponds to a fitted multiplicative change of $0.980$ in delay per doubling of width, and the interval to factors between $0.557$ and $1.425$; over the full $16\\to256$ range the fitted factors are $0.924$ with interval $[0.096, 4.12]$.\n138:Verdict under pre-declared rule & inconclusive \\\\\n284:We tested whether the grokking delay of a one-layer transformer on modular addition decreases with width, using a pre-declared, censoring-aware slope test with a seed-cluster bootstrap interval. The sealed evaluation was valid and gave a slope of $-0.020$ with interval $[-0.585, 0.354]$ over widths $16$--$256$ and four seeds, so the prediction is neither supported nor refuted. Forty percent of runs were censored at the step limit, the interval covers about two-fifths of the slopes that the delay floor and cap allow, and a four-cluster bootstrap has only $35$ distinct resamples. A single-seed reference trainer gave $-0.368$, inside the interval. We therefore do not claim that delay depends on width, nor that it does not, and we do not attribute the difference between the reference and the evaluated model to any of its modifications. The practical lesson is a measurement one: for a delay statistic with a floor and a censoring cap, report per-width delays and censoring flags, use enough seeds that the cluster bootstrap has a rich resampling support, and treat the attainable slope range as part of the result. The per-width records, the isolating ablation of the multipliers, and sweeps over training fraction and weight decay would turn this inconclusive result into a testable one.\n386:Large language models acted as autonomous agents in this work: they proposed the candidate directions, wrote and modified the evaluated model's code, drafted the analysis scripts, and wrote this manuscript. The task environment supplied the evaluators, the workload manifests, and the decision rule for the width-slope test; the models did not edit them. No results were produced by an LLM other than by executing the code described above, and all numbers in the paper come from the evaluator records or from the derivations in \\texttt{scripts/derive\\_resolution.py}.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a22feb4e-b707-4639-9c1a-fc2d3121b3c0","uuid":"ec1c5ac4-eea9-48f9-a159-6812d003f3a3","timestamp":"2026-11-11T14:52:23.625Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"final.evidence.episodes {'candidate_diagnostics_trust': 'unverified', 'episode_count': 4, 'evaluation_scope': 'sealed_final', 'id': 'final.evidence.episodes', 'json_pointer': '/episodes', 'manifest_path': '<HOME>/.herdr/\nfinal.metric.censored_fraction {'evaluation_scope': 'sealed_final', 'id': 'final.metric.censored_fraction', 'manifest_path': '<WORKTREES>/experiment/.project/runs/agentic-scientist-t02-20261111T08\nfinal.metric.combined_score {'evaluation_scope': 'sealed_final', 'id': 'final.metric.combined_score', 'manifest_path': '<WORKTREES>/experiment/.project/runs/agentic-scientist-t02-20261111T08555\nfinal.metric.control_max_val_acc {'evaluation_scope': 'sealed_final', 'id': 'final.metric.control_max_val_acc', 'manifest_path': '<WORKTREES>/experiment/.project/runs/agentic-scientist-t02-20261111T\nfinal.metric.grok_delay_log_slope {'evaluation_scope': 'sealed_final', 'id': 'final.metric.grok_delay_log_slope', 'manifest_path': '<WORKTREES>/experiment/.project/runs/agentic-scientist-t02-20261111\nfinal.metric.grok_delay_slope_ci_high {'evaluation_scope': 'sealed_final', 'id': 'final.metric.grok_delay_slope_ci_high', 'manifest_path': '<WORKTREES>/experiment/.project/runs/agentic-scientist-t02-2026\nfinal.metric.grok_delay_slope_ci_low {'evaluation_scope': 'sealed_final', 'id': 'final.metric.grok_delay_slope_ci_low', 'manifest_path': '<WORKTREES>/experiment/.project/runs/agentic-scientist-t02-20261\nfinal.metric.missing_episodes {'evaluation_scope': 'sealed_final', 'id': 'final.metric.missing_episodes', 'manifest_path': '<WORKTREES>/experim