{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EpUywwhGitwV2AXYCpM8TQ","type":"tool_result","content":"37:Delay is right-censored at $10{,}000$ steps, and a pre-declared rule reads the 95\\% seed-cluster bootstrap interval of the slope of $\\ln(\\text{delay})$ against $\\log_2(\\text{width})$ over widths $16$--$256$ with four seeds.\n51:\\paragraph{Contributions.} (i) We state precisely the delay statistic, its floor and censoring cap, the seed-cluster slope test, and the three-way decision rule that the sealed evaluation uses (Section~\\ref{sec:method}). (ii) We report the measured outcome \n68:\\paragraph{Relation to this work.} Rather than propose a new explanation, we examine what a pre-declared, censoring-aware slope test on one architecture and one task can and cannot resolve, and we report an inconclusive result in full.\n83:\\paragraph{Test statistic and decision rule.} With widths $d\\in\\{16,32,64,128,256\\}$ and seeds $s\\in\\{201,202,203,204\\}$ the statistic is the ordinary-least-squares slope $\\beta$ of $\\ln\\Delta_{d,s}$ on $\\log_2 d$ over the $20$ pairs. Its interval is the 2.\n85:\\paragraph{Caveat on few clusters.} The cluster-robust inference literature documents that inference with a small number of clusters can be unreliable, and that refinements to the basic cluster bootstrap, such as the wild cluster bootstrap-$t$, can improve \n123:The slope of $\\ln\\Delta$ on $\\log_2 d$ is $-0.020$, with 95\\% seed-cluster bootstrap interval $[-0.585,\\,0.354]$. Because the interval contains zero, the pre-declared rule returns \\emph{inconclusive}: the prediction that delay decreases with width is neith\n133:\\toprule\n135:\\midrule\n138:Verdict under pre-declared rule & inconclusive \\\\\n144:\\bottomrule\n151:\\includegraphics[width=0.8\\textwidth]{figures/slope_interval_vs_decision_rule.pdf}\n177:\\toprule\n179:\\midrule\n190:\\bottomrule\n208:\\toprule\n210:\\midrule\n215:\\midrule\n217:\\bottomrule\n245:\\toprule\n247:\\midrule\n252:\\bottomrule\n266:\\toprule\n268:\\midrule\n276:\\bottomrule\n284:We tested whether the grokking delay of a one-layer transformer on modular addition decreases with width, using a pre-declared, censoring-aware slope test with a seed-cluster bootstrap interval. The sealed evaluation was valid and gave a slope of $-0.020$ \n305:\\toprule\n307:\\midrule\n318:\\bottomrule\n336:\\toprule\n338:\\midrule\n344:\\bottomrule\n366:\\toprule\n368:\\midrule\n372:\\bottomrule\n386:Large language models acted as autonomous agents in this work: they proposed the candidate directions, wrote and modified the evaluated model's code, drafted the analysis scripts, and wrote this manuscript. The task environment supplied the evaluators, the\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nDelayed generalization (``grokking'') on modular arithmetic has been explained through weight norm, circuit efficiency, and a lazy-to-rich transition in training dynamics; here we ask how the delay depends on model width, a quantity that these accounts relate to scale-dependent knobs.\nWe test one falsifiable prediction in a one-layer transformer trained with AdamW (weight decay $1.0$, full batch) on modular addition modulo $97$: that the delay between fitting the training pairs and generalizing to held-out pairs shrinks as width grows.\nDelay is right-censored at $10{,}000$ steps, and a pre-declared rule reads the 95\\% seed-cluster bootstrap interval of the slope of $\\ln(\\text{delay})$ against $\\log_2(\\text{width})$ over widths $16$--$256$ with four seeds.\nThe measured slope is $-0.020$ with interval $[-0.585,\\,0.354]$, so the verdict is \\emph{inconclusive}; $40\\%$ of the $20$ runs were censored, and the interval covers about two-fifths of the slopes that the delay floor and cap permit.\nA single-seed reference trainer gives $-0.368$, inside the interval.\nThe evaluation itself was valid, so the result is a valid but inconclusive measurement rather than a failed run; the interval comes from only four seed clusters and its coverage is not guaranteed. We state what the result cannot say and which measurements would resolve it.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a22feb4e-b707-4639-9c1a-fc2d3121b3c0","uuid":"101b5b22-416e-4304-a99d-32a8d974cf8d","timestamp":"2026-11-11T14:52:25.988Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"37:Delay is right-censored at $10{,}000$ steps, and a pre-declared rule reads the 95\\% seed-cluster bootstrap interval of the slope of $\\ln(\\text{delay})$ against $\\log_2(\\text{width})$ over widths $16$--$256$ with four seeds.\n51:\\paragraph{Contributions.} (i) We state precisely the delay statistic, its floor and censoring cap, the seed-cluster slope test, and the three-way decision rule that the sealed evaluation uses (Section~\\ref{sec:method}). (ii) We report the measured outcome \n68:\\paragraph{Relation to this work.} Rather than propose a new explanation, we examine what a pre-declared, censoring-aware slope test on one architecture and one task can and cannot resolve, and we report an inconclusive result in full.\n83:\\paragraph{Test statistic and decision rule.} With widths $d\\in\\{16,32,64,128,256\\}$ and seeds $s\\in\\{201,202,203,204\\}$ the statistic is the ordinary-least-squares slope $\\beta$ of $\\ln\\Delta_{d,s}$ on $\\log_2 d$ over the $20$ pairs. Its interval is the 2.\n85:\\paragraph{Caveat on few clusters.} The cluster-robust inference literature documents that inference with a small number of clusters can be unreliable, and that refinements to the basic cluster bootstrap, such as the wild cluster bootstrap-$t$, can improve \n123:The slope of $\\ln\\Delta$ on $\\log_2 d$ is $-0.020$, with 95\\% seed-cluster bootstrap interval $[-0.585,\\,0.354]$. Because the interval contains zero, the pre-declared rule returns \\emph{inconclusive}: the prediction that delay decreases with width is neith\n133:\\toprule\n135:\\midrule\n138:Verdict under pre-declared rule & inconclusive \\\\\n144:\\bottomrule\n151:\\includegraphics[width=0.8\\textwidth]{figures/slope_interval_vs_decision_rule.pdf}\n177:\\toprule\n179:\\midrule\n190:\\bottomrule\n208:\\toprule\n210:\\midrule\n215:\\midrule\n217:\\bottomrule\n245:\\toprule\n247:\\midrule\n252:\\bottomrule\n266:\\toprule\n268:\\midrule\n276:\\bottomrule\n284:We tested whether the grokking delay of a one-layer transformer on modular addition decreases with width, using a pre-declared, censoring-aware slope test with a seed-cluster bootstrap interval. The sealed evaluation was valid and gave a slope of $-0.020$ \n305:\\toprule\n307:\\midrule\n318:\\bottomrule\n336:\\toprule\n338:\\midrule\n344:\\bottomrule\n366:\\toprule\n368:\\midrule\n372:\\bottomrule\n386:Large language models acted as autonomous agents in this work: they proposed the candidate directions, wrote and modified the evaluated model's code, drafted the analysis scripts, and wrote this manuscript. The task environment supplied the evaluators, the\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nDelayed generalization (``grokking'') on modular arithmetic has been explained through weight norm, circuit efficiency, and a lazy-to-rich transition in training dynamics; here we ask how the delay depends on model width, a quantity that these accounts relate to scale-dependent knobs.\nWe test one falsifiable prediction in a one-layer transformer trained with AdamW (weight decay $1.0$, full batch) on modular addition modulo $97$: that the delay between fitting the training pairs and generalizing to held-out pairs shrinks as width grows.\nDelay is right-censored at $10{,}000$ steps, and a pre-declared rule reads the 95\\% seed-cluster bootstrap interval of the slope of $\\ln(\\text{delay})$ against $\\log_2(\\text{width})$ over widths $16$--$256$ with four seeds.\nThe measured slope is $-0.020$ with interval $[-0.585,\\,0.354]$, so the verdict is \\emph{inconclusive}; $40\\%$ of the $20$ runs were censored, and the interval covers about two-fifths of the slopes that the delay floor and cap permit.\nA single-seed reference trainer gives $-0.368$, inside the interval.\nThe evaluation itself was valid, so the result is a valid but inconclusive measurement rather than a failed run; the interval comes from only four seed clusters and its coverage is not guaranteed. We state what the result cannot say and which measurements would resolve it.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
