{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014tZVgdqKaX9deLWrtb5mBY","type":"tool_result","content":"\\paragraph{Test statistic and decision rule.} With widths $d\\in\\{16,32,64,128,256\\}$ and seeds $s\\in\\{201,202,203,204\\}$ the statistic is the ordinary-least-squares slope $\\beta$ of $\\ln\\Delta_{d,s}$ on $\\log_2 d$ over the $20$ pairs. Its interval is the 2.5--97.5 percentile range of $2{,}000$ bootstrap slopes in which seeds are resampled with replacement and each resampled seed contributes all five of its widths (a cluster bootstrap over seeds, with a fixed resampling seed; \\citealp{Cameron2015APG}). The decision rule is declared before the final evaluation: the prediction ``delay decreases with width'' is \\emph{supported} if the interval lies entirely below zero, \\emph{refuted} if it lies at or above zero, and \\emph{inconclusive} if it straddles zero or the evidence is incomplete. The claim is restricted to the tested setting (modular addition, one training fraction, AdamW with weight decay $1.0$, full batch, one layer, the five widths) and makes no statement about other operations, moduli, optimizers, depths, or larger models.\n\\paragraph{Caveat on few clusters.} The cluster-robust inference literature documents that inference with a small number of clusters can be unreliable, and that refinements to the basic cluster bootstrap, such as the wild cluster bootstrap-$t$, can improve coverage \\citep{Cameron2008BootstrapBasedIF,Cameron2015APG}. We use the plain percentile interval with four clusters as pre-declared, make no claim of nominal coverage, and treat the interval as a coarse summary of seed-to-seed variability rather than an exact confidence statement.\nThe slope of $\\ln\\Delta$ on $\\log_2 d$ is $-0.020$, with 95\\% seed-cluster bootstrap interval $[-0.585,\\,0.354]$. Because the interval contains zero, the pre-declared rule returns \\emph{inconclusive}: the prediction that delay decreases with width is neither supported nor refuted. A point estimate of $-0.020$ corresponds to a fitted multiplicative change of $0.980$ in delay per doubling of width, and the interval to factors between $0.557$ and $1.425$; over the full $16\\to256$ range the fitted factors are $0.924$ with interval $[0.096, 4.12]$.\nVerdict under pre-declared rule & inconclusive \\\\\nLarge language models acted as autonomous agents in this work: they proposed the candidate directions, wrote and modified the evaluated model's code, drafted the analysis scripts, and wrote this manuscript. The task environment supplied the evaluators, the workload manifests, and the decision rule for the width-slope test; the models did not edit them. No results were produced by an LLM other than by executing the code described above, and all numbers in the paper come from the evaluator records or from the derivations in \\texttt{scripts/derive\\_resolution.py}.\n77:\\paragraph{Delay.} Training runs for at most $T=10{,}000$ full-batch steps and is probed every $250$ steps, giving $40$ checkpoints at which accuracy is computed by the evaluator on all pairs. Let \n91:The evaluated configuration is a one-layer, four-head transformer with width $d$ (the model dimension), an MLP of hidden size $4d$ with ReLU, no normalization layers, a learned embedding for the $9\n111:\\paragraph{Validity.} The evaluator marks the final evaluation valid (\\texttt{qualified}, status \\texttt{completed}) when, according to its source, all episodes completed, the compute and call bud\n156:\\subsection{Resolution of the design}\n158:An inconclusive verdict is informative only relative to the effects the design could have resolved. Three properties of the protocol, all computed from its constants and the reported interval (Tab\n229:For context, three configurations that differ cumulatively by the three modifications of Section~\\ref{sec:model} were evaluated on the separate development plane with two seeds each, with scores $\n256:\\paragraph{Threshold and time-grid sensitivity.} The delay is read off a $250$-step grid with a $0.99$ threshold on both accuracies, so it is blind to differences below one probe interval and sens\n322:The final evaluation made $1{,}804$ calls to the evaluated model (limit $2{,}200$; $22$ episodes, each a reset, a data load, and $40$ train/predict pairs) and took $11{,}764$\\,s of wall-clock time\n323:% @evidence[[derived/resolution.json#compute]] @evidence[[source_evidence/final/final_manifest.json#compute_usage]]\n382:The accompanying files are: the evaluated model's source and the two evaluators (\\texttt{source\\_evidence/}); the aggregate and per-seed final records (\\texttt{source\\_evidence/final/}); \\texttt{s","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a22feb4e-b707-4639-9c1a-fc2d3121b3c0","uuid":"f383fea0-ce98-4c5f-a50d-9db4f83798fa","timestamp":"2026-11-11T14:52:27.765Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"\\paragraph{Test statistic and decision rule.} With widths $d\\in\\{16,32,64,128,256\\}$ and seeds $s\\in\\{201,202,203,204\\}$ the statistic is the ordinary-least-squares slope $\\beta$ of $\\ln\\Delta_{d,s}$ on $\\log_2 d$ over the $20$ pairs. Its interval is the 2.5--97.5 percentile range of $2{,}000$ bootstrap slopes in which seeds are resampled with replacement and each resampled seed contributes all five of its widths (a cluster bootstrap over seeds, with a fixed resampling seed; \\citealp{Cameron2015APG}). The decision rule is declared before the final evaluation: the prediction ``delay decreases with width'' is \\emph{supported} if the interval lies entirely below zero, \\emph{refuted} if it lies at or above zero, and \\emph{inconclusive} if it straddles zero or the evidence is incomplete. The claim is restricted to the tested setting (modular addition, one training fraction, AdamW with weight decay $1.0$, full batch, one layer, the five widths) and makes no statement about other operations, moduli, optimizers, depths, or larger models.\n\\paragraph{Caveat on few clusters.} The cluster-robust inference literature documents that inference with a small number of clusters can be unreliable, and that refinements to the basic cluster bootstrap, such as the wild cluster bootstrap-$t$, can improve coverage \\citep{Cameron2008BootstrapBasedIF,Cameron2015APG}. We use the plain percentile interval with four clusters as pre-declared, make no claim of nominal coverage, and treat the interval as a coarse summary of seed-to-seed variability rather than an exact confidence statement.\nThe slope of $\\ln\\Delta$ on $\\log_2 d$ is $-0.020$, with 95\\% seed-cluster bootstrap interval $[-0.585,\\,0.354]$. Because the interval contains zero, the pre-declared rule returns \\emph{inconclusive}: the prediction that delay decreases with width is neither supported nor refuted. A point estimate of $-0.020$ corresponds to a fitted multiplicative change of $0.980$ in delay per doubling of width, and the interval to factors between $0.557$ and $1.425$; over the full $16\\to256$ range the fitted factors are $0.924$ with interval $[0.096, 4.12]$.\nVerdict under pre-declared rule & inconclusive \\\\\nLarge language models acted as autonomous agents in this work: they proposed the candidate directions, wrote and modified the evaluated model's code, drafted the analysis scripts, and wrote this manuscript. The task environment supplied the evaluators, the workload manifests, and the decision rule for the width-slope test; the models did not edit them. No results were produced by an LLM other than by executing the code described above, and all numbers in the paper come from the evaluator records or from the derivations in \\texttt{scripts/derive\\_resolution.py}.\n77:\\paragraph{Delay.} Training runs for at most $T=10{,}000$ full-batch steps and is probed every $250$ steps, giving $40$ checkpoints at which accuracy is computed by the evaluator on all pairs. Let \n91:The evaluated configuration is a one-layer, four-head transformer with width $d$ (the model dimension), an MLP of hidden size $4d$ with ReLU, no normalization layers, a learned embedding for the $9\n111:\\paragraph{Validity.} The evaluator marks the final evaluation valid (\\texttt{qualified}, status \\texttt{completed}) when, according to its source, all episodes completed, the compute and call bud\n156:\\subsection{Resolution of the design}\n158:An inconclusive verdict is informative only relative to the effects the design could have resolved. Three properties of the protocol, all computed from its constants and the reported interval (Tab\n229:For context, three configurations that differ cumulatively by the three modifications of Section~\\ref{sec:model} were evaluated on the separate development plane with two seeds each, with scores $\n256:\\paragraph{Threshold and time-grid sensitivity.} The delay is read off a $250$-step grid with a $0.99$ threshold on both accuracies, so it is blind to differences below one probe interval and sens\n322:The final evaluation made $1{,}804$ calls to the evaluated model (limit $2{,}200$; $22$ episodes, each a reset, a data load, and $40$ train/predict pairs) and took $11{,}764$\\,s of wall-clock time\n323:% @evidence[[derived/resolution.json#compute]] @evidence[[source_evidence/final/final_manifest.json#compute_usage]]\n382:The accompanying files are: the evaluated model's source and the two evaluators (\\texttt{source\\_evidence/}); the aggregate and per-seed final records (\\texttt{source\\_evidence/final/}); \\texttt{s","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
